  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Five innings of nothing, then the Padres found their swing when it mattered most.",
    "what_this_means": "Hart lasted only 2.2 innings, but the bullpen didn't blink. Griffin Canning threw three scoreless, and the string of relievers that followed. Peralta, Rodriguez, Estrada, Miller. Allowed nothing and struck out nine in 5.1 innings combined. That's the kind of shutdown that makes a thin offense hold up. The Padres scored three in the fourth to take the lead and never relinquished it, converting their one real opportunity while the bullpen sealed every door after. This is how a team with a .234 average survives: the pitching gets tight, the bats get just enough, and the last reliever closes it without ceremony. They're now five straight wins deep, and the formula is becoming clear. When both sides click, they're harder to move than their record suggests. Three runs proved to be exactly enough because the back end wouldn't allow more.",
    "what_to_watch": "San Francisco visits Petco tonight with TBD on the mound against JP Sears, who'll look to keep the streak alive in a division contest that carries weight these teams don't ignore.",
